{"kind":"assert","text":"NF-5 source review at 9ea739ea9d102f3972558ec2429071a185f94e23, under request c288c917 and NF-7 ruling657e4217. This is a builder source review, not checker approval. No source changes, browser launch, compiler, test, control, formatter, generator, build or gate.\n\nTwo material store findings:\n1. packages/client/src/browser-custody.ts:158-168 stores an identity's entire spilled outcome history as one IndexedDB array. segmented(id,from,limit) gets/structured-clones the entire value before slicing, despite CustodyStore's bounded-slice contract at custody.ts:207 and the bounded DECIDED_SLICE caller at411-417. Repeated lost resends of the same signing can grow history without reaching the eight-signing bound. segment also copies/rewrites the whole history at159-162. Proposed repair: ordered outcome-position keys plus tiny length metadata; transactional truncate/append and a bounded key-range/cursor read, preserving exact outcome counts and rollback. Witness the real IndexedDB request shape on a late small page after enough actual Custody retries to spill history; verify all outcomes and final settlement survive. Do not use a fake IndexedDB adapter as the sole boundedness proof.\n2. browser-custody.ts:113-117 maps an existing live/archive row with missing/non-string text to null. read at136 also maps stored text \"null\" to a null record. Custody.#read at335-338 then reports absence and #kept at439-450 may build/sign a replacement under that key. Null must mean no stored row. Existing malformed storage must throw unreadable/StoreFormatError. Add a real IndexedDB witness that corrupts a kept wrapper or stores text \"null\", then calls actual Custody.send/find and proves zero replacement build/POST with the original row retained. The four store stand-in tests contain no malformed-storage case.\n\nOther concrete lifecycle/availability risks:\n- browser-custody.ts:95 caches an IDB connection, but close at176 closes only the BroadcastChannel/observers; Database at36 has no close/versionchange handling. A live tab that calls store.close() still blocks deleteDatabase/version upgrade. A pending write can recreate the channel at153 after close. Define close semantics and close resolved/pending connections, with versionchange handling; a real-browser close/delete completion witness distinguishes this from observer-only cleanup.\n- BroadcastChannel construction/post at81/153 can reject write after the transaction committed at140-150 and read-back succeeded at151. That misreports a successful durable write as storage failure (custody.ts:368/351-359). Isolate optional notification failures after durable success; test throwing notification transport without failing committed custody.\n- browserRequests.send checks current only before awaiting custody admission (browser-request.ts:119-122). The supplied join/Counter builders enter signedIntent without a fresh current check (browser-joining.ts:149-151; counter/browser/client.ts:84-87). If the exclusive admission/read is held and the context ends, the old signer still runs before its post-sign check rejects. The deliver current guard prevents a later act POST, but does not prevent this late signing. Wrap build with current checks before/after its await. Also browser-request.ts:87-90/99-100 can invoke recorded after current ends during a POST/read; browser-joining.ts:90-98 then signs and posts a fresh session request without another current check. Gate those async boundaries; test held admission and held reply with context invalidation, zero new signer/session calls, no slot clear and no obsolete UI outcome.\n\nDurability qualification: transactions await complete and verify record read-back, and archive moves share one readwrite transaction. They use the default durability hint at browser-custody.ts:103, not strict. IndexedDB's default completion does not promise a stable-storage flush; strict is still a UA hint. The current join reload witness proves reload recovery, not power-loss/crash durability. Do not describe it as the latter. Relevant primary references: https://www.w3.org/TR/IndexedDB/ and https://www.w3.org/TR/web-locks/.\n\nValidation closure after source repair and a root-granted slot:\n- Run only affected client compiler contexts and browser-custody/browser-request/browser-joining/custody focused owners first; Counter's changed gateway/storage/voice/app owners and the Lending owners also remain in NF-5's final closure. Then one gate at the fixed head, with sealed external evidence.\n- Extend the real Chromium join harness for actual multi-tab record/exclusive locks, transaction-abort rollback and the store cases above. Existing browser-custody.test.ts:1-4 explicitly says its stand-in writes immediately and abort rolls nothing back; it cannot prove IDB atomicity. Preserve the actual lost-reply/reload journal equality witness.\n- NF-5 changes covered voice lifecycle/recovery source but leaves verification/state-machines/coverage.json unchanged. Reconcile the Voice model/source correspondence and recorded real-source witness/control before claiming that boundary remains checked; the project skill requires a reserved slot. Observation ownership is a separate narrow property, not proof of custody persistence.\n- Historical evidence stays historical: the Chromium join ran at6839776002 plus witness files; Counter Node witnesses passed52 cases. Current main.ts production entry paths share counterCustody(), database dap-counter-custody-1 and Web Lock dap-counter-custody. The explicitly scripted preview.ts:60-61 uses memoryStore and is not browser durability evidence.\n- Per657e4217, the Counter hosted half remains NF-7, not missing NF-5 source. No local adapter/second local Counter witness is proposed. After NF-5 lands and planner deploys/installs Counter, run custody-witness.mjs with the actual screen address and private invitation. It must prove one unknown retained record after lost reply; reload+Check settlement; one Counter POST; one signing/actor/key; one journal act whose signed bytes equal custody. Before that run, installed counterEnrollment, applicationScreenTransport and installed main.ts gateway calls remain browser-uncovered. Failure reopens NF-5.\n\nI recommend correcting the two material store findings before the NF-5 gate and deciding the lifecycle/close contract above before source. No repair is claimed by this review."}